# Auth & Roles

OfficeFlow has two roles: **Manager** and **Tenant**. Always use `OfficeFlow.Application.Common.Security.Roles` constants — never raw role strings.

## API layer

- Default: `[Authorize]` on controllers that require login.
- Manager-only mutations/admin reads: `[Authorize(Roles = Roles.Manager)]`.
- Tenants must not access other tenants' data: compare claim tenant id vs requested id; return `Forbid()` on mismatch.
- Controllers extract actor identity/role from claims and pass them into commands when authorization is part of the use case (e.g. cancel).

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD
[Authorize(Roles = Roles.Manager)]
public async Task<ActionResult> Confirm(...) { ... }

if (User.IsInRole(Roles.Tenant))
{
    var actorTenantId = GetTenantIdFromClaims();
    if (actorTenantId is null || actorTenantId != tenantId)
        return Forbid();
}

// ❌ BAD
[Authorize(Roles = "manager")]
if (role == "Tenant") { ... }
```

## Application layer

- Prefer validating actor role/ownership in FluentValidation or the handler (e.g. `CancelReservationCommand` with `ActorRole` + `ActorTenantId`).
- Do not trust client-sent "I am a manager" flags without claims-backed values from the API.

## Frontend

- Protect manager-only UI with the same role model; never rely on hiding buttons alone for security — API must enforce.
